<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry II, Duke of Guise</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1614">1614</a>–<a href="/years/1664">1664</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-guise">House of Guise</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry II, Duke of Guise, was a French noble of the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> who claimed the Angevin inheritance to the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. Invited by the rebels of the Neapolitan Republic, he arrived in Naples in late 1647 and took the title of duke of the republic. Spanish forces retook the city in April 1648 and captured him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-ii-duke-of-guise.jpg" alt="Henry II, Duke of Guise" width="320" height="540" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anthony van Dyck · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anthonis_van_Dyck_-_Portrait_of_Henri_II_de_Lorraine.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henri was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 4 April 1614, the second son of Charles, Duke of Guise, and Henriette Catherine de Joyeuse. At the age of fifteen he became Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>, and according to Gédéon Tallemant des Réaux he had a well-known affair with the actress Marguerite Béguin during this time. The death of his eldest brother Francis in 1639 brought him the dukedom the following year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Guise opposed Richelieu and conspired with the Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>, fighting at the Battle of La Marfée in 1641. Condemned to death, with his property seized for lèse-majesté, he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>; he was reprieved, returned in 1643 and recovered his property.</p><p>Hoping to make good his family's ancient claims to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, he joined the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masaniello">Masaniello</a> in 1647. The Royal Republic of Naples appealed to the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and was nominally headed by Guise, with the title of doge in imitation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. He soon alienated the Neapolitans by his lack of tact and had little influence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-mazarin">Cardinal Mazarin</a>. Captured by the Spaniards when the republic fell in 1648, he was held until 1652. A second attack on Naples in 1654 failed, partly because an English fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-blake">Robert Blake</a> supported the Spanish.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He then settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> as Grand Chamberlain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, went deeply into debt on horses and entertainments, and was the patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-corneille">Pierre Corneille</a>, whom he lodged in the Hôtel de Guise. Two women claimed to be his wife: Anna Gonzaga, who in 1639 appears to have been duped by a clandestine ceremony, and Honorée de Berghes, Countess of Bossut, who said she had married him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> on 11 November 1641. The Sacra Rota declared that marriage valid in March 1666, but the king and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-guise">House of Guise</a> refused to recognise the decision, so she received nothing of his fortune.</p><p>Anna Gonzaga wrote that he had the figure, the air and the manners of a hero in a novel, especially charming in conversation and gifted at making himself loved, but flighty in his attachments, inconstant in his projects and hasty in carrying things out. Henri died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 2 June 1664 and was succeeded by his nephew Louis Joseph de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henri II, Duke of Guise” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They invited <b>Henry II, Duke of Guise</b>, a French claimant to the Angevin inheritance, who arrived in November and took the title Duke of the Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/neapolitan-republic-1647-1648">Neapolitan Republic (1647–1648)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1646" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Henry II, Duke of Guise"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5132 5945h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5132 5945h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1646">What was here in 1646? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
